-- title:
--   Theorem 2.3 — the value is $\operatorname{Var}(\mu)(A_t)+\bar\mu^\top B_t\bar\mu+D_t^\top\bar\mu+\mathbb E[F_t]$; optimal strategy jumps only at $t$, $T$ (corrected: (2.16), BSDE class)
-- statement:
--   For all $\gamma_1,\gamma_2,\beta,\rho,\lambda$ there is $\alpha_0>0$ such that the following holds for every $\alpha$ with the standing assumption and with
--   $$\lambda=0\qquad\text{or}\qquad\big(\lambda\rho\gamma_2>0\ \text{and}\ \alpha<\alpha_0\big),$$
--   and for every probability space carrying a horizon $T>0$, a Brownian motion $W$, a square-integrable initial position $x_0$ independent of $W$, the augmented filtration generated by $x_0$ and $W$, and a progressively measurable volatility $\sigma$ with $\mathbb E\int_0^T\sigma^2<\infty$:
--
--   0. the coefficient equations (2.14), (2.15), (2.16) and the BSDE (2.17) have a solution $(A,B,D,F,Z^F)$;
--
--   and for every such solution, every $t\in[0,T)$ and every $\mathcal F_t$-measurable square-integrable initial state $\mathcal X=(X_{t-},Y_{t-},C_{t-})$ with law $\mu$ and mean $\bar\mu$:
--
--   1. the value function (2.12) is
--   $$V(t,\mathcal X)=\operatorname{Var}(\mu)(A_t)+\bar\mu^\top B_t\bar\mu+D_t^\top\bar\mu+\mathbb E[F_t];\qquad(2.20)$$
--   2. there is an admissible strategy $\tilde Z$ whose state liquidates ($\tilde X_T=0$) and attains $J(t,\tilde Z)=V(t,\mathcal X)$, which jumps only at the beginning and the end of the trading period, with
--   $$\Delta\tilde Z_t=-\frac{I^A_t}{\tilde a}(\mathcal X_{t-}-\bar\mu)-\frac{I^B_t}{a}\bar\mu-\frac{I^D_t}{a},\qquad \Delta\tilde Z_T=X_{T-},\qquad(2.21)$$
--   and which satisfies the dynamics (2.22) on $(t,T)$.
--
--   This is the main result of the paper: the value of the mean-field liquidation problem with semimartingale strategies is linear-quadratic in the law of the initial state, and the optimal strategy is explicit, with block trades only at the two ends of the trading period.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: (2.16), first line of Ḋ₁, second bracket: −2λγ₂ + 2(γ₁α − γ₂ρ)B₁₁ + γ₁γ₂α + αγ₂B₁₃ → the same bracket with 2αγ₂B₁₃ (the first bracket X₁ of (2.15)); reason: with the printed αγ₂B₁₃ the identity 2I^DI^B/a = 2𝒢ᵀB + Ḋᵀ + Dᵀℋ + Dᵀℋ̄ used in the proof of Proposition 4.1 (p.20) leaves the residual αB₁₃(−αγ₁D₁ + γ₂ρD₁ − αγ₂D₃ + αγ₁γ₂E[x₀])/(2γ₂(αγ₁ − γ₂ρ − λ)) ≠ 0; counterexample with the Fig. 2 parameters (ρ = 0.4, γ₁ = 0.1, γ₂ = 0.5, α = 1.8, β = 3, λ = 0, E[x₀] = 1, T = 1, all assumptions hold): printed D(0) = (0.2248, 0.0436), corrected D(0) = (0.0983, 0.0475), so the printed (2.20) is false for one of two initial states differing only in E[C_{t−}]
--   - correction: F a real-valued adapted square integrable process (p.9) solving (2.17) → F adapted and integrable, with M = ∫Z^F dW a true martingale (SolvesF); reason: σ is only assumed square integrable, so the driver term σ²(2A₁₁ − γ₂)/(2γ₂²) is only integrable; counterexample: σ_s = h(x₀) constant in time with E h² < ∞ = E h⁴ and λ = 0, where 2A₁₁ − γ₂ < 0 on [0, T) (Ȧ₁₁ = ρA₁₁²/γ₂ > 0, A₁₁,T = γ₂/2), so F_t = E[∫_t^T g ds | 𝓕_t] contains h²·c(t) with c(t) ≠ 0 and is not square integrable; the integrable class with a true-martingale stochastic integral has exactly one solution F_t = E[∫_t^T g ds | 𝓕_t]
--   - reading: α is small enough → ∃ α₀ > 0 depending only on (γ₁, γ₂, ρ, λ, β), and the claim for every α < α₀; reason: the smallness in the proof (pp.13–15) comes only from M̃ ⪰ 0, which involves exactly these constants; this is the stronger reading (α₀ uniform in T and the random data)
--   - reading: 𝔽 the augmented Brownian filtration, x₀ a square integrable r.v. independent of W → 𝓕_s = σ(x₀) ∨ σ(W_r : r ≤ s) ∨ {P-null measurable sets}; reason: x₀ is independent of W yet must be 𝓕₀-measurable (it is the initial inventory, and the initial jump (2.21) at t = 0 depends on it); adjoining σ(x₀) at time 0 is the only reading under which the problem is posed
--   - reading: σ a square integrable progressively measurable process → σ progressively measurable on [0, T] with E∫₀ᵀ σ_s² ds < ∞ (σ may vanish); reason: the standard meaning of square integrability for a progressive integrand on [0, T]
--   - reading: J(t, Z) = E[∫_t^T (Y_{s−} dZ_s + (γ₂/2) d[Z]_s + σ_s d[Z, W]_s) + ∫_t^T λX_s² ds] → the realized cost Y_{t−}Z_t + (γ₂/2)Z_t² + ∫_{(t,T]} Y_{s−} dZ_s + (γ₂/2)[Z]_{(t,T]} + [Z, ∫σ dW]_{(t,T]} + ∫_t^T λX_s² ds; reason: the integral includes the initial jump at t (Z_{t−} = 0, (4.1) p.16, which integrates over [t, T)); ∫σ d[Z, W] = [Z, ∫σ dW] is the Kunita–Watanabe identity
--   - encoding: in terms of the processes A, B, D, F introduced in (2.14)–(2.17) → solutions of (2.14)–(2.17) exist, and (i)–(ii) hold for every solution tuple; reason: the coefficients are unique (Theorem 3.1, Remark 3.2, and the uniqueness of the linear ODE and BSDE), so this is the same claim
--   - encoding: V(t, 𝒳) = inf_{Z ∈ 𝒜_t} J(t, Z) subject to (2.10) → V an infimum in EReal over runs satisfying IsRun and the liquidation constraint X_T = 0; J a Bochner expectation; reason: the EReal infimum presupposes no boundedness (an empty or unbounded-below feasible set would give ⊤ or ⊥, not a junk 0), and the theorem asserts the real value
--   - encoding: the Itô integral ∫σ dW of a progressive integrand → IsItoIntegral: N continuous 𝔽-martingale, N₀ = 0, [N, N] = ∫σ² ds, [N, W] = ∫σ ds; reason: these brackets force [N − ∫σ dW] = 0, so N = ∫σ dW; left-point Riemann sums (HasStochIntegral) are valid only for càdlàg integrands
--   - encoding: the state (2.10)/(2.18) driven by Z, and the integrals in the cost → a Run bundles Z, the state 𝒳, N = ∫σ dW, ∫Y₋ dZ, [Z] and [Z, N], characterized by IsRun; the state equation is the integral form of (2.18) on [t, T]; reason: each of these objects exists and is unique up to indistinguishability for Z ∈ 𝒜_t, so a quantifier over runs is a quantifier over strategies
--   - encoding: processes on [0, T] with the state started at time t from 𝒳_{t−} = 𝒳 → processes on ℝ≥0; Z_s = 0 and 𝒳_s = 𝒳 for s < t, everything after T irrelevant; deterministic coefficients are functions ℝ → ℝ; reason: the time index of the reused KurtzProtter91 / EthierKurtz definitions; changes no instance
--   - reading: The optimal strategy Z̃ jumps only at the beginning and the end of the trading period → there is an admissible liquidating run attaining V(t, 𝒳) with the jumps (2.21), no jump in (t, T) and the dynamics (2.22); it is not asserted that every optimal strategy has this form; reason: Section 4 identifies Z̃ as the strategy given by (2.21) and (2.22) (p.16, first sentence; p.20) and proves that this strategy is optimal; the paper neither states nor proves more
--   - reading: the random initial state 𝒳 = (X_{t−}, Y_{t−}, C_{t−}) at time t (p.8), for which the page states no class → 𝒳 𝓕_t-measurable with square-integrable components (Model.IsInitialState); reason: (2.21) makes ΔZ̃_t a function of 𝒳, so an adapted strategy needs 𝓕_t-measurability, and Var(μ)(A_t) in (2.20) and E[[Z]_T] < ∞ in (2.11) need second moments; no other class makes the statement meaningful
--   - encoding: E[[Z]_T] < ∞ in (2.11), where [Z] includes the atom (ΔZ_t)² at t → E[Z_t² + [postTrades]_T] < ∞, with postTrades the trades strictly after t, and Z càdlàg for every ω; reason: Z_{t−} = 0, so the atom is Z_t², which a dyadic bracket started at 0 misses when t = 0; 𝔽 contains the null sets, so an a.s. càdlàg strategy has an everywhere càdlàg modification
--   - encoding: dZ̃_s = (…) ds − (I^A_s/ã)𝒟_s dW_s on (t, T), (2.22), with the time derivatives İ^A, İ^B, İ^D → Z̃_s − Z̃_r = ∫_r^s drift du + L_s − L_r a.s. for all t < r ≤ s < T, with L = ∫−(I^A/ã)𝒟 dW (IsItoIntegral) and the derivatives as deriv at interior points; reason: this is the integral form of the differential; I^A, I^B, I^D are differentiable on (0, T) because A, B, D solve (2.14)–(2.16)
-- source:
--   Fu, Horst & Xia, A Mean-Field Control Problem of Optimal Portfolio Liquidation with Semimartingale Strategies, arXiv:2207.00446v3, Theorem 2.3, (2.20)–(2.22), p.11; the model (2.9)–(2.12), p.8, and the standing assumption, p.10

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MFCLiquidation_Params
import Definitions.Def_MFCLiquidation_Riccati
import Definitions.Def_MFCLiquidation_IsItoIntegral
import Definitions.Def_MFCLiquidation_Model

universe u

open MeasureTheory
open scoped NNReal

namespace MFCLiquidation

/-- **Theorem 2.3**, arXiv:2207.00446v3, p.11, the main result of the paper. Assume the standing
assumption, and either `λ = 0`, or `λργ₂ > 0` and `α` small enough (below a threshold `α₀` depending
only on `γ₁, γ₂, ρ, λ, β`). Then the coefficients `A, B, D, F` of (2.14)–(2.17) exist, and for every
solution, every `t ∈ [0, T)` and every square-integrable `𝓕_t`-measurable initial state `𝒳`, with
`μ` the law of `𝒳`:

* (i) `V(t, 𝒳) = Var(μ)(A_t) + μ̄ᵀB_tμ̄ + D_tᵀμ̄ + E[F_t]`, (2.20);
* (ii) there is an admissible liquidating strategy `Z̃` attaining `V(t, 𝒳)`, with initial jump
  (2.21), terminal jump `X_{T−}`, no jump in `(t, T)`, and the dynamics (2.22) on `(t, T)`.

(2.16) is taken corrected, and (2.17) in the integrable class (`SolvesF`). -/
theorem theorem_2_3 (γ₁ γ₂ β ρ lam : ℝ) :
    ∃ α₀ : ℝ, 0 < α₀ ∧ ∀ α : ℝ,
      let p : Params := ⟨γ₁, γ₂, α, β, ρ, lam⟩
      p.Standing → (lam = 0 ∨ (0 < lam * ρ * γ₂ ∧ α < α₀)) →
      ∀ (Ω : Type u) [MeasurableSpace Ω] (D : Model Ω), D.Assumption →
        (∃ c : Coeffs Ω, c.Solves p D) ∧
        ∀ c : Coeffs Ω, c.Solves p D →
          ∀ t : ℝ≥0, t < D.T → ∀ ξ : Ω → Fin 3 → ℝ, D.IsInitialState t ξ →
            V p D t ξ = (c.valueFormula p D t (D.P.map ξ) : EReal) ∧
            ∃ R : Run Ω, Run.IsRun p D t ξ R ∧ Run.Liquidates D R ∧ c.IsCandidate p D t ξ R ∧
              (Run.J p D t ξ R : EReal) = V p D t ξ := by sorry

end MFCLiquidation
